  this repo doesn't have, and — more to the point — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a classic script with no module loader.
  There is no existing config layer to change, so introducing one means deciding how a second file gets loaded and what
  interface it exposes. That's structure, not a one-line edit. So: questions, approaches, a design you approve section
  by section, then a spec.

  First question, and it's the one that drives everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "easier to change environments" can mean the page figures out its own environment, or it can mean there's
  exactly one obvious line a human edits before deploying. That choice decides whether the settings module contains
  logic or just data, and it's expensive to reverse later because consumers end up shaped around it.

  - [1mAuto-detect from hostname[0m — ship one identical file to every environment; nothing to remember at deploy time. Costs
    a small mapping table, and adding an environment means editing that table. Rules out nothing.
  - [1mSingle edit point[0m — module exports one [38;5;153mCURRENT_ENV[39m you change by hand. Simplest possible thing, zero logic to test.
    Cost: it's still a manual step, and the wrong value silently ships to production.
  - [1mRuntime override[0m — [38;5;153m?env=[39m query param or [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, layered over a default. Great for testing prod builds against
    staging. Cost: real code to write, and anyone can point your page at any backend.

  [1mRecommendation: auto-detect from hostname.[0m Your stated pain is the effort of changing environments, and only
  auto-detect removes the step entirely rather than relocating it. There's no build step here to inject a value, so
  hostname is the only signal actually available at runtime. Option 3 is cheap to add on top of it later if you ever
  need it — I'd leave it out now.
